<Project>

  <Import Project="..\..\Directory.Packages.props" />

  <!--
    Every version this project uses is the repository's; the one thing it does differently is not pin
    its transitive ones. This is the same file Quartz.Benchmark carries, for the same reason and with
    one more: EF Core 10 — which TickerQ.EntityFrameworkCore and Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL
    both pull in — asks for Microsoft.Extensions.* patches above the 10.0.0 floors Quartz ships, and
    central transitive pinning holds them at the floor, so restore refuses the graph with NU1109.

    A VersionOverride in the csproj would answer that for this project and break every measured run
    instead: BenchmarkDotNet generates a project per job beside the built exe, copies the package
    references into it and builds it, and the generated project gets the centrally defined version
    while this one asks for the override — a downgrade either way round. The generated project sits
    under bin/, so a file here is what reaches both. That is the failure #3816 found on
    Quartz.Benchmark, where every out-of-process case came back NA.

    Raising the floors is not on the table: a floor is a demand every consumer of every shipped package
    inherits. This project ships to nobody, so its transitive versions are its own business.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>false</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
